# auth.md

NanoGPT supports agent and app access through user-approved API keys. OAuth-aware clients should use authorization-code + PKCE. Agents can also use the browser-approved device login flow for CLI-style handoff.

Resource server: `https://nano-gpt.com/api/v1`
Issuer origin: `https://nano-gpt.com`

## 1. Discover

Fetch protected resource metadata:

```http
GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource HTTP/1.1
Host: nano-gpt.com
```

The metadata points to:

- Protected resource metadata: `https://nano-gpt.com/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`
- Agent registration metadata: `https://nano-gpt.com/.well-known/nanogpt-agent-auth`
- OAuth authorization server metadata: `https://nano-gpt.com/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server`
- Agent capability manifest: `https://nano-gpt.com/api/v1/agent-capabilities`
- API docs: `https://nano-gpt.com/api`
- MCP setup: `https://nano-gpt.com/mcp`

NanoGPT also returns this discovery hint on Chat Completions authentication failures:

```http
WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="https://nano-gpt.com/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource"
```

## 2. Pick A Method

Use one of these methods:

- OAuth key handoff shortcut: Redirect the user to `https://nano-gpt.com/auth?callback_url=...` with S256 PKCE, then exchange the code at `https://nano-gpt.com/api/v1/auth/keys`.
- OAuth PKCE: Redirect the user to `https://nano-gpt.com/oauth/authorize`, then exchange the code at `https://nano-gpt.com/oauth/token`.
- Existing API key: If the user already has a NanoGPT API key, send it as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.
- User-claimed device login: If the user needs a key, start a device login and ask the user to approve it in the browser.

NanoGPT does not currently support anonymous spend-capable credentials. Agent-verified ID-JAG registration is not enabled yet.

## 3. Scopes

- `models.read`: Read the model catalogs and prices as the account sees them (the public catalogs need no key).
- `api.use`: Run models and spend the account balance or subscription allowance: chat, responses, messages, completions, images, video, audio, embeddings, rerank, moderation, batches, paid tools (web search, scraping, transcription and similar) and their uploads, status and result endpoints, plus the model catalogs. Bounded by the balance and the key's spending limits. A paid response states its cost and may state the remaining balance. Also the chat handoff (`POST /api/extension/conversation-handoff`): the posted conversation is held for 15 minutes and is saved only when the user opens the returned import link in a browser signed in to the same account. It cannot read or change stored conversations.
- `balance.read`: Read the account balance, subscription allowance and the calling key's own limits and settings.
- `usage.read`: Read usage and spending history, including per-request usage records.
- `payments.write`: Create deposits to the account (deposit addresses, crypto payment requests, payment-required flows) and read their status. Cannot withdraw or move funds out.
- `providers.read`: List the account's own provider keys (provider, key suffix, status) and route options that depend on them. Cannot add, replace or read a key.
- `feedback.write`: File private bug, feature and model-quality reports through the feedback API and read the reports this key filed. Cannot read other support tickets.

Scopes are enforced (`scope_enforcement: enforced` in the discovery metadata). A key issued through OAuth, the key handoff shortcut or `POST /api/v1/auth/keys/code` can do only what its granted scopes allow, and the consent screen lists exactly those permissions. The scopes are read from the grant stored with the key; nothing a client sends can widen them. A wider scope needs a new authorization, which the user approves again and which returns a new key.

A request outside the grant gets `403` with `error.code: "insufficient_scope"`, the missing scope in `error.required_scope`, and `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="insufficient_scope", scope="..."`. Send the user through authorization again with that scope added.

Some endpoints are never open to a connected app, whatever it was granted: creating, listing or revealing API keys, sign-in and security settings, adding or removing provider keys, account settings, invitations, reading or changing stored conversations and characters, the eval workspace, support tickets, and anything that moves funds out. They answer `403` with `error.code: "owner_session_required"`. An endpoint that is not listed under a scope behaves the same way.

What each scope opens:

- `api.use`: `/api/v1/chat/completions`, `/responses`, `/messages`, `/completions`, `/embeddings`, `/images/*`, `/audio/*`, `/moderations`, `/rerank`, `/batches`, `/files`, `/memory`, video and image generation with their upload, status and result endpoints, the paid tools (web search, scraping, transcription and similar), the model catalogs, and `POST /api/extension/conversation-handoff` (offers the user a conversation to import; it is kept for 15 minutes and saved only when the user opens the returned `import_url` in a browser signed in to the same account).
- `models.read`: the model catalogs as the account sees them (`/api/v1/models`, `/api/personalized/v1/models`, `/api/models/{id}/providers`).
- `balance.read`: `/api/check-balance`, `/api/subscription/v1/usage`, `/api/subscription/v1/credit-status`, `/api/v1/api-keys/current`. The Nano deposit address in the balance reply needs `payments.write`.
- `usage.read`: `/api/v1/usage`, `/api/v1/usage/requests/{id}` and the `/api/usage/*` history of the calling key.
- `payments.write`: `/api/deposit/*`, `/api/transaction/create/*`, `/api/transaction/status/*`, `/api/check-nano-balance`, `/api/receive-nano`, `/api/check-receivables`.
- `providers.read`: `GET /api/user/provider-keys`.
- `feedback.write`: `/api/v1/feedback`.

Running a model spends the user's balance, within the spending limits and expiry the user set for the key on the consent screen. OAuth authorization requests must include `api.use`; a request without it is rejected with `invalid_scope`. The public model catalogs (`GET /api/v1/models`, `/api/v1/image-models`, `/api/v1/video-models`, `/api/v1/audio-models`) need no key.

Transition: keys issued before 2026-11-17 were approved while scopes were informational. Until 2027-01-05 a call outside their grant still works and is recorded; from that date they are held to their scopes too. If your app uses balance, usage, deposit or provider-key endpoints, request those scopes and have your users connect again before then. Both dates are in the discovery metadata under `x-nanogpt-scope-enforcement`.

Device login is different: it hands the user's CLI an ordinary NanoGPT API key with the full access of a key created in settings, and its approval page says so. Scopes do not apply to it, or to keys users create themselves.

## 4. OAuth Key Handoff Shortcut

For clients that want a short browser-based key handoff, build a URL like:

```text
https://nano-gpt.com/auth?callback_url=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%3A8000%2Fcallback&code_challenge=...&code_challenge_method=S256&state=...
```

`callback_url` must be HTTPS, or loopback HTTP with an explicit port. NanoGPT requires S256 PKCE for this shortcut. Optionally add `invitation_code` (a referral link code, the part after `/r/` in `https://nano-gpt.com/r/<code>`) to attribute accounts created during this sign-in; see Referral Attribution below. After approval, exchange the returned code:

```http
POST /api/v1/auth/keys HTTP/1.1
Host: nano-gpt.com
Content-Type: application/json
```

```json
{
  "code": "...",
  "code_verifier": "..."
}
```

Success response includes both `key` and OAuth-style `access_token` fields:

```json
{
  "key": "sk-nano-...",
  "access_token": "sk-nano-...",
  "token_type": "Bearer",
  "scope": "models.read api.use",
  "user_id": "..."
}
```

Authenticated clients can also create a one-time authorization code for a downstream app:

```http
POST /api/v1/auth/keys/code HTTP/1.1
Host: nano-gpt.com
Authorization: Bearer sk-nano-...
Content-Type: application/json
```

```json
{
  "redirect_uri": "http://127.0.0.1:8000/callback",
  "code_challenge": "...",
  "code_challenge_method": "S256",
  "limit": 20,
  "usage_limit_type": "weekly",
  "expires_at": "2099-07-01T00:00:00.000Z",
  "key_label": "Local coding agent"
}
```

`limit` is USD and can be `daily`, `weekly`, or `monthly`. `key_label`, `limit`, and `expires_at` are applied to the dedicated API key when the code is exchanged.

The response includes top-level fields and a nested `data` object:

```json
{
  "id": "...",
  "code": "...",
  "app_id": "ngpt_callback_...",
  "user_id": "...",
  "expires_at": "...",
  "data": {
    "id": "...",
    "code": "...",
    "app_id": "ngpt_callback_...",
    "user_id": "...",
    "expires_at": "..."
  }
}
```

## 5. OAuth PKCE With Dynamic Registration

Register a public PKCE client:

```http
POST /oauth/register HTTP/1.1
Host: nano-gpt.com
Content-Type: application/json
```

```json
{
  "client_name": "my-agent",
  "redirect_uris": ["http://127.0.0.1:8000/callback"],
  "grant_types": ["authorization_code"],
  "response_types": ["code"],
  "token_endpoint_auth_method": "none"
}
```

Build an authorization URL with `response_type=code`, `client_id`, exact `redirect_uri`, `scope`, `state`, `code_challenge`, and `code_challenge_method=S256`. After approval, exchange the returned code:

```http
POST /oauth/token HTTP/1.1
Host: nano-gpt.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
```

```text
grant_type=authorization_code&client_id=ngpt_...&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%3A8000%2Fcallback&code=...&code_verifier=...
```

Success response:

```json
{
  "access_token": "sk-nano-...",
  "token_type": "Bearer",
  "scope": "models.read api.use"
}
```

The access token is a dedicated NanoGPT API key for that app and user. It can spend from the user's NanoGPT balance until the app revokes it (see Revocation below) or the user disconnects the app or constrains the key in settings.

### Referral Attribution

Both `https://nano-gpt.com/auth` and `https://nano-gpt.com/oauth/authorize` accept an optional `invitation_code` parameter. Pass your referral link code (the part after `/r/`) to have users who create a NanoGPT account during your sign-in flow referred by you:

- Attribution only applies to accounts created within the last hour that have no referrer yet. Existing accounts keep their current attribution, including people who already used NanoGPT without an account and only create one now.
- Users can decline the referral on the consent screen without affecting app access. They get 5% off eligible usage; you earn 10% of eligible usage charges and 5% of paid subscriptions, credited daily. [Referral rules and exclusions](https://nano-gpt.com/invitations#referral-earnings) apply. Attribution is account-wide and permanent, including after disconnecting your app.
- An invitation code that carries credit adds that credit to the user's personal balance on approval, whether or not the account is new.
- Codes may be at most 256 characters; a longer value is rejected with `invalid_request`. Otherwise, invalid, expired, or ineligible codes never block authorization; the user still receives a normal authorization code.

## 6. Register With User-Claimed Device Login

Start a login request:

```http
POST /api/cli-login/start HTTP/1.1
Host: nano-gpt.com
Content-Type: application/json
```

```json
{
  "client_name": "my-agent"
}
```

Success response:

```json
{
  "device_code": "opaque-device-code",
  "user_code": "ABCD-2345",
  "verification_uri": "https://nano-gpt.com/cli-login/verify",
  "verification_uri_complete": "https://nano-gpt.com/cli-login/verify?code=ABCD-2345",
  "expires_in": 600,
  "interval": 2
}
```

Tell the user to open `verification_uri_complete` and approve the request. Then poll:

```http
POST /api/cli-login/poll HTTP/1.1
Host: nano-gpt.com
Content-Type: application/json
```

```json
{
  "device_code": "opaque-device-code"
}
```

While the user has not approved yet, NanoGPT returns:

```json
{
  "status": "authorization_pending"
}
```

After approval, NanoGPT returns the API key exactly once:

```json
{
  "status": "approved",
  "key": "sk-nano-..."
}
```

Store the key securely and use it as:

```http
Authorization: Bearer sk-nano-...
```

## 7. Use The Credential

Call NanoGPT APIs with bearer auth. The primary agent surfaces are:

- `POST /api/v1/chat/completions`
- `POST /api/v1/responses`
- `POST /api/v1/messages`
- `GET /api/v1/models`
- `GET /api/v1/image-models`
- `GET /api/v1/video-models`
- `GET /api/v1/audio-models`
- `GET /api/v1/agent-capabilities`

For the full endpoint matrix, fetch `https://nano-gpt.com/api/v1/agent-capabilities`.

## 8. Errors

| Endpoint | Error | Meaning | Agent action |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `/auth` | `invalid_request` | The callback URL or PKCE challenge is invalid | Rebuild the authorization URL |
| `/auth` | `rate_limited` | Too many requests (HTTP 429) | Wait `Retry-After` seconds and retry |
| `/oauth/register`, `/oauth/token`, `/oauth/revoke` | `invalid_request` with `error_description: rate_limited` | Too many requests (HTTP 429) | Wait `Retry-After` seconds and retry |
| `/auth`, `/oauth/register`, `/oauth/token`, `/oauth/revoke` | `temporarily_unavailable` | Request protection or the service is briefly unavailable (HTTP 503) | Wait `Retry-After` seconds and retry |
| `/oauth/revoke` | `invalid_request` | The `token` parameter is missing | Send the access token as `token` |
| `/oauth/revoke` | `unauthorized_client` | The token was issued to a different `client_id` | Send the `client_id` the token was issued to, or omit it |
| `/api/v1/auth/keys` | `invalid_grant` | The code is expired, already used, or the PKCE verifier does not match | Restart OAuth |
| `/oauth/authorize` | `invalid_request` | The request is missing a required OAuth parameter, has an invalid redirect URI, or does not use PKCE S256 | Rebuild the authorization URL |
| `/oauth/token` | `invalid_grant` | The code is expired, already used, or the PKCE verifier does not match | Restart OAuth |
| `/api/cli-login/start` | `rate_limited` | Too many login attempts | Wait `Retry-After` seconds and retry |
| `/api/cli-login/start`, `/api/cli-login/poll` | `temporarily_unavailable` | Login protection is briefly unavailable (HTTP 503) | Wait `Retry-After` seconds and retry; keep polling until the device code expires |
| `/api/cli-login/poll` | `rate_limited` | Polling too fast | Wait `Retry-After` seconds and poll again |
| `/api/cli-login/poll` | `authorization_pending` | User has not approved yet | Wait `interval` seconds and poll again |
| `/api/cli-login/poll` | `expired` | Device code expired | Restart the device login flow |
| `/api/cli-login/poll` | `consumed` | Key was already returned | Restart if the key was not saved |
| `/api/cli-login/poll` | `key_revoked` | Approved key was revoked before retrieval | Restart registration |
| API endpoints | `missing_api_key` | No bearer credential | Register or ask user for a key |
| API endpoints | `invalid_api_key` | Credential is invalid, inactive, expired, or blocked | Drop the key and restart registration |
| API endpoints | `api_key_origin_not_allowed` | Browser Origin is not allowed for that key | Ask the user to update key settings |

## 9. Revocation

Apps that received their key through OAuth (`/oauth/token`) or the key handoff shortcut (`/api/v1/auth/keys`) can revoke it themselves, for example on sign-out or when the key may have leaked (RFC 7009):

```http
POST /oauth/revoke HTTP/1.1
Host: nano-gpt.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
```

```text
token=sk-nano-...&client_id=ngpt_...
```

`client_id` is optional; when present it must be the client the key was issued to. `token_type_hint` is accepted and ignored. NanoGPT answers HTTP 200 with an empty body whether or not the token was valid or already revoked, so the call is safe to repeat. Revoking deactivates the key and ends the app's grant: new requests with the key fail at once. The user's request history for the key is kept.

Keys from device login and keys users create themselves can't be revoked through this endpoint. Users can delete, block, expire, or constrain any API key, and disconnect any app, from NanoGPT settings. If a previously working key returns 401, discard it and restart discovery.
